\details{
\subsection{Before you start: the model weights and their license}{

TabPFN is a \emph{pre-trained} model. Instead of estimating parameters from your
data, \code{brulee_tab_pfn()} feeds your training data, together with the rows to
predict, to a large neural network that was trained in advance on millions
of synthetic data sets. That network's parameters (its "weights") are made
by the company Prior Labs and are \strong{not} included in brulee: you download
them once, and Prior Labs requires that you accept their license first.

The license allows free use for non-commercial purposes, such as research,
teaching, and evaluation. Commercial or production use requires a separate
license from Prior Labs (\href{mailto:sales@priorlabs.ai}{sales@priorlabs.ai}). Read the license when you
accept it.

Each model version has its own license. Accepting the TabPFN-3.5 license
covers \code{"v3.5"} (the default) and \code{"v3.5-fast"}; \code{"v3"} needs the TabPFN-3
license.
\subsection{One-time setup}{
\enumerate{
\item Create a free account at \url{https://ux.priorlabs.ai}.
\item On the \strong{Licenses} tab, accept the license for each model version you
plan to use (TabPFN-3.5 and/or TabPFN-3).
\item On your account page, copy your \strong{API key}. Treat it like a password.
\item Give R the key by adding a line to your \code{.Renviron} file (open it with
\code{usethis::edit_r_environ()}), then restart R:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{TABPFN_TOKEN=paste-your-api-key-here
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}
\item Download the weights for the version you will use:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{brulee::tab_pfn_download_weights("v3.5")
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

This checks your key and license with Prior Labs, then downloads the
weights from Hugging Face: about 880 MB for \code{"v3.5"}, 330 MB for
\code{"v3.5-fast"}, and 450 MB for \code{"v3"} (one file each for classification
and regression). \code{tab_pfn_weights_available()} tells you whether they
are already downloaded.
}

In an interactive session you can skip steps 4 and 5: the first time
\code{brulee_tab_pfn()} needs weights that aren't downloaded, it offers to
download them, opens the Prior Labs login page in your browser, and asks you
to paste your API key. The key is then saved in \verb{~/.cache/tabpfn/auth_token}
for later sessions. Non-interactive sessions (scripts, R Markdown and
Quarto documents, continuous integration) need \code{TABPFN_TOKEN}; if the
weights are missing there, \code{brulee_tab_pfn()} stops with an error that says
how to download them.
}

\subsection{After the setup}{

The weights are stored in a per-user cache (see
\code{\link[=tab_pfn_download_weights]{tab_pfn_download_weights()}}) and used from there, with no further contact
with Prior Labs or internet access. Remove them with \code{\link[=tab_pfn_clear_cache]{tab_pfn_clear_cache()}},
for example to free disk space,
or because you stop using the model, as the license requires when it
ends.

If you have used the Python \code{tabpfn} package on this computer, it may
already have downloaded the weights and saved your API key; brulee uses
both, so you may have nothing to do.
}

}

\subsection{Computing requirements}{

The model runs on the CPU or, when available, on a CUDA GPU (or an Apple
GPU with \code{device = "mps"}). On the CPU it accepts at most 5,000 training
rows; see "Data size limits" below.
}

\subsection{Differences from the Python package}{

The computations follow the Python \code{tabpfn} package (version 9.1.0), but
the random choices of the ensemble members use R's random number generator
(so \code{\link[=set.seed]{set.seed()}} makes fits reproducible), and predictions differ slightly
from Python's. For v3, Python computes the SVD features with a randomized
SVD when the training data has more than a million cells; brulee always
uses the exact SVD.

Do not use \code{brulee_tab_pfn()} and the Python-based \pkg{tabpfn} package in
the same R session: R torch and Python torch can't be loaded in one process.
}
}
\section{Data size limits}{


Each model version was trained for data up to a certain size, and
\code{brulee_tab_pfn()} refuses larger data with an error that names the count
and the limit. Rows are training rows.\tabular{lllll}{
   Version \tab Rows (GPU) \tab Rows (CPU) \tab Predictors \tab Classes \cr
   \code{"v3"} \tab 1M \tab 5K \tab 2K \tab 160 \cr
   \code{"v3.5"} \tab 1M \tab 5K \tab 20K \tab 160 \cr
   \code{"v3.5-fast"} \tab 1M \tab 5K \tab 20K \tab 160 \cr
}


The CPU column applies whenever the model runs on the CPU (including
\code{device = NULL} on a machine without a CUDA GPU). Above a fifth of that
limit, \code{brulee_tab_pfn()} warns that prediction may be slow. Set
\code{ignore_pretraining_limits = TRUE} to lift the row and predictor limits;
the model then runs on data larger than it was trained for, more slowly
and possibly less accurately. The class limit can't be lifted. Use
\code{training_set_limit} to fit on a sample instead.

Memory grows with the number of rows times the number of predictors and
the ensemble size: on the CPU, 5,000 training rows with 50 predictors and
8 ensemble members need about 12 GB. The row and predictor maxima trade
off against each other, so you cannot always reach both at once. For
\code{"v3.5"}, Prior Labs recommends up to 6,000 predictors even though the
model accepts 20,000. See \url{https://docs.priorlabs.ai/models}.
}

